A team of Nepali Sherpas – led by Italian filmmaker and explorer Leonardo Avezzano – carried the kite to ensure that the dreams of children in the besieged Strip could make it “to the top of the world”, Salameh told Al Jazeera from the Everest base camp last week. Salameh, who has previously summited Everest, stayed at the first base camp due to frostbite and a blood clot in his left hand. The 56-year-old climber launched this expedition to raise $10m towards medical aid for children in the Strip and draw global attention towards the difficulties they have faced during the Israeli genocide in Gaza. “After months of preparation, sacrifice, training, fear, hope, prayers, and carrying the weight of a message much bigger than themselves… the kite carrying the dreams of the children of Gaza is now flying above the highest point on Earth,” Salameh said in a video posted to Instagram. “From the rubble and pain of Gaza … to the roof of the world. A dream refused to die,” he wrote in the caption. Avezzano, who has documented the journey to the summit, and his team were hailed by Salameh.

